  },
  "articleBody": "It's super frustrating to see mold creeping up on your basement drywall, right? A lot of times, this happens because moisture wasn't properly addressed before installation. One of the biggest mistakes is attaching drywall directly to concrete without a proper moisture barrier and framing. Concrete is porous, so it can wick moisture straight into your drywall, creating a perfect environment for mold growth.\n\nHere’s what you need to consider:\n1.  **Moisture Assessment is Key:** Before anything else, test your basement walls and floor for moisture. You can do a simple test by taping a plastic square to the concrete for a week during a wet season to see if condensation forms underneath.\n2.  **Framing and Barriers:** You can't just put drywall on concrete. You need a framed wall with an air gap, and often a vapor or moisture barrier to protect the drywall from the concrete.\n3.  **Mind the Gap:** Drywall should never touch the concrete slab. Always leave a ½- to 1-inch gap at the bottom to prevent moisture wicking. This gap usually gets covered by baseboards.\n4.  **Choose the Right Materials:** While standard drywall is cheaper, mold- and moisture-resistant panels are often a smarter choice for basements, given the inherent humidity.\n\nIf you're dealing with persistent moisture issues or want to ensure your basement drywall stands the test of time, it's wise to get a professional involved. They can properly assess your basement's specific needs and implement the right solutions.",
